---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro'
import BlogPostShell from '../../components/BlogPostShell.astro'
import BlogToolEmbed from '../../components/BlogToolEmbed.astro'

const slug = 'shoe-size-converter'
const seo = {
  title: 'How to convert shoe sizes between US, UK, EU, and Brazil | maratool',
  description:
    'Convert shoe, sneaker, and tennis shoe sizes across US, UK, EU, Brazil, Japan, and cm — and learn the formulas behind every conversion. Free size chart.',
  canonical: `https://maratool.com/blog/${slug}`,
  schema: {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BlogPosting',
    headline: 'How to convert shoe sizes between US, UK, EU, and Brazil',
    image: 'https://maratool.com/og/converter.svg',
    datePublished: '2026-07-18',
    dateModified: '2026-07-18',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Marcell Almeida', url: 'https://marcell.com.br' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'maratool',
      url: 'https://maratool.com',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://maratool.com/favicon.svg' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `https://maratool.com/blog/${slug}` },
    url: `https://maratool.com/blog/${slug}`,
    description:
      'Convert shoe sizes across US, UK, EU, Brazil, Japan, and cm — and learn the formulas behind every conversion.',
  },
  breadcrumbSchema: {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://maratool.com' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://maratool.com/blog' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'How to convert shoe sizes between US, UK, EU, and Brazil', item: `https://maratool.com/blog/${slug}` },
    ],
  },
}
---
<Base {...seo}>
  <Layout>
    <BlogPostShell
      title="How to convert shoe sizes between US, UK, EU, and Brazil"
      lead="Enter a size you already know and read the equivalent in every other country — instantly."
      date="July 18, 2026"
      dateIso="2026-07-18"
    >
      <p>Buying shoes, sneakers, or tennis shoes from a foreign store means decoding a size you have never used. The <a href="/shoe-size-converter">Shoe Size Converter</a> maps any size to US, UK, EU, Brazil, Japan, Mexico, Australia, and centimeters for men, women, and kids — with a Standard and a Sneakers fit.</p>

      <BlogToolEmbed slug="shoe-size-converter" title="Try it — pick a system and enter your size" height={520} />

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Choose a category</strong> — men, women, or kids. US, UK, and Australian numbering differ by gender; EU, Brazil, and cm do not.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick the system you know</strong> — US, UK, EU, or even your foot length in centimeters.</li>
        <li><strong>Read every equivalent</strong> — the result cards and the full chart update together.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>The formulas behind it</h2>
      <p>Every system is a fixed function of foot length. EU uses the Paris point (one size = <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> cm), so <code>EU = 1.5 × foot cm + 3</code>. UK, US, and Australia use the barleycorn (one size = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> inch), anchored so US men 9 ≈ 27 cm. Brazil runs about two points below EU, and Japan is simply your foot length in centimeters. The <a href="/shoe-size-converter#methodology">methodology section</a> on the tool spells out each one.</p>

      <h2>Tennis shoe and sneaker size conversion</h2>
      <p>The converter defaults to the US Brannock scale — the foot measurer used in shoe stores. Tennis shoes and athletic sneakers often size a little differently, so we checked five brands against the standard. They do not fully agree, which is exactly why a single "sneaker size" or "tennis shoe size" is a myth. All rows below are for the same men’s foot of 26&nbsp;cm.</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Source</th><th>US men</th><th>EU</th><th>Women (same foot)</th><th>Men→women</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Brannock (US standard)</strong></td><td>8</td><td>40</td><td>9</td><td>+1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Nike</td><td>8</td><td>41</td><td>9.5</td><td>+1.5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Puma</td><td>8</td><td>40.5</td><td>9.5</td><td>+1.5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ASICS</td><td>8</td><td>41.5</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>New Balance</td><td>8</td><td>41.5</td><td>9</td><td>+1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Adidas</td><td>~8</td><td>~41.7</td><td>—</td><td>+1 / +1.5</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Two patterns hold across every brand: the men’s US number maps to the same foot length (US 8 ≈ 26&nbsp;cm everywhere), and EU runs half a point to a point above the Brannock standard. But the men-to-women gap splits: Nike and Puma use +1.5, New Balance uses +1, and Adidas’ own pages give both. The <strong>Sneakers</strong> toggle on the tool applies the common Nike/Puma convention; switch it off for the Brannock standard. Sources: <a href="https://www.nike.com/size-fit/mens-footwear" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Nike</a>, and compiled brand charts on <a href="https://runrepeat.com/size-charts/nike" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">RunRepeat</a>.</p>

      <h2>Why measuring beats guessing</h2>
      <p>Because brands disagree by up to a size, the safest input is your actual foot length. Measure heel to longest toe in centimeters, select <strong>cm</strong>, and let the converter do the rest. These are ISO / Mondopoint standard values, not any single brand’s chart.</p>

      <hr class="blog-divider" />
      <p class="blog-footer-note">The <a href="/shoe-size-converter">Shoe Size Converter</a> is free, runs locally, and never sends your data to a server. More <a href="/converter">converter tools</a> at <a href="/">maratool.com</a>.</p>
    </BlogPostShell>
  </Layout>
</Base>
